# The files both machines touch.
#
# CLAUDE.md and SECOND-DEVELOPER.md both say these six need a word before they
# are edited, because a change in any of them lands on the other machine's work
# without warning. Until now that rule was prose in two documents with nothing
# behind it: nothing asked, nothing checked, and it was forgotten as often as
# it was kept.
#
# A leading slash pins the pattern to the repository root, the same way a
# gitignore pattern works. Without it, tools/app/Shell.cs would also match any
# Shell.cs in any folder added later.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT DO. GitHub requests a review from the owner named
# here when a pull request touches the file. It does not require one until
# Require review from Code Owners is switched on in branch protection for main,
# and GitHub never asks a pull request's own author for a code owner review. So
# a pull request Patrick opens on Shell.cs asks Djinn, and one Djinn opens asks
# Patrick, which is exactly the intent. Patrick's own work goes straight to
# main and never becomes a pull request at all, so this binds Djinn's branches
# and the occasional pull request Patrick does open.
#
# A mistyped path or a handle without push access is ignored in silence, which
# is why the check is worth running after any edit here:
#
#     gh api repos/EvilPatrick06/Thornswood/codeowners/errors

/tools/app/Shell.cs        @EvilPatrick06 @WiccanDjinn
/tools/app/Checks.cs       @EvilPatrick06 @WiccanDjinn
/tools/app/Keep.cs         @EvilPatrick06 @WiccanDjinn
/tools/app/Hold.cs         @EvilPatrick06 @WiccanDjinn
/tools/app/Updater.cs      @EvilPatrick06 @WiccanDjinn
/tools/Publish-Update.ps1  @EvilPatrick06 @WiccanDjinn

# Not on CLAUDE.md's list, and here because getting them wrong is the same
